Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||06-06-2001||01:49 PM||||his skid marks were 15 feet (less than one car lenght BTW)<P> but it took 130 feet for the brakes to lock up<BR> <BR>  total = 145 (c:<P> for my '85SE its about 8.9 S 0 to 60, and about the same 60 to 0. Thinking of installing an anchor on a chain on the front hood, with a remote 'drop' switch on the center console.<P>  see my post on vacuum assist failures - Im working on it<P>   My question would be, can you guys lock up all four wheels when you want to on dry pavement? at 30? at 60?<P>  I did read in my owners manual that "the brake system on the Fiero is designed to prevent the wheels from locking up easily"<P>  for a while i thought, gee maybe this is how my brakes are suppose to be<P>  more likely the owners manual is full of weasle words to mask the fact Fieros have weak brakes.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||06-06-2001||06:42 PM||||One reason most people can't lock up the brakes in a Fiero is that they just aren't strong enough. I can do it fine, but I weigh 250 rock solid pounds.<P>Fieros also have quite a small brake booster. That's one of the prices you pay for a marvelously low hoodline and good forward visibility.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||06-07-2001||10:11 AM||||in my case, once the pedal hits the floor it dont matter how much harder you push it, or how much you weigh - still working on it.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Master_Sushi||06-07-2001||11:43 AM||||With my 14 inch tech wheels runing snow tires (only for winter) on my 88 coupe I can easily lock up the brakes any time I want. With my summer 16 inch wheels and tires its a bit harder, Ive only locked them up at low speeds. But the brakes on my fiero are some of the best on any car ive driven. I've never noticed any fade on them, and I do work them hard once and a while  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">.||||reg||10
Z||000010||elfiero||06-07-2001||01:05 PM||||My 84 cpe had all stock brakes with all NEW<BR>parts-rotors,calipers,pads.This dumb c*nt<BR>pulls out RIGHT in front of me-I locked them<BR>up alright-hard enough to flex the calipers<BR>to the point that the pistons "cocked" in the<BR>bores and the front wheels remained locked<BR>up until I removed the wheels and hit the<BR>calipers with a softfaced hammer.No, I did'nt<BR>hit her -but the raghead b*tch thought I was<BR>one crazy white guy when I started yelling at<BR>her!(it was so close she thought we touched)<BR>The moral of this tale is that I got<BR>rid of the aluminum calipers and went to grandam brakes all the way around.Next up;<BR>13" rotors and 4piston calipers!!!<p>[This message has been edited by elfiero (edited 06-07-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000011||88formula||06-07-2001||04:42 PM||||The grand AM calipers are big heavy clunky cast iron units compared to the light aluminum fiero calipers. That means more unsprung weight. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Shiner||06-07-2001||11:04 PM||||George Bush says  "Aluminum good, steel baaad!  Wouldn't be prudent at this juncture."||||reg||1
Z||000013||elfiero||06-08-2001||10:09 AM||||I guess my next question would be:which is <BR>worse,unsprung weight or woefully inadiquate<BR>brakes?.IMHO,if there is one major design<BR>flaw in our beloved little cars,it would be<BR>the brake system.If you drive the car to it's<BR>full cornering potential,you will imeadeately<BR>find out you don't have NEAR ENOUGH brakes<BR>for the job!!.Now,I know this is'nt for <BR>everyone, but with upgraded drivetrain(about<BR>300HP,300ftlbs torque,better clutch)upgraded<BR>brakes,17" wheels&tires,and the normal susp.<BR>upgrades,you now own a world class super car<BR>that will spank just about anything that is<BR>street legal!!.The best part is you can do <BR>this for very few bucks-if you try.I did all<BR>of the above for less than the tax and lic.<BR>on a new C5!!(this includes the<BR>purchase price of a ratted '87GT)<p>[This message has been edited by elfiero (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000014||Ken Wittlief||06-08-2001||11:10 AM||||with all the fiero based kits cars out there im really surprized nobody has come out with a brake upgrade kit<P>  brakes on my saturns are all super - and you can get antilocks - they all stop on a dime.||||reg||1
Z||000015||sjp777||06-09-2001||11:11 AM||||How about this brake upgrade? and yes it is even available for the 88's. Time to start saving pennies.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/brake13.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/brake13.htm</A> <P>4 piston aluminum calipers with SEPERATE mechanical calipers for the E-brake<BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_4.jpg">  <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A><p>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 06-09-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000016||Will||06-10-2001||09:57 PM||||I just trashed my parking brake (it's NOT and emergency brake--wouldn't have done any good in an emergency) in favor of Grand Am/Beretta/Sunbird front brakes on the rear.<BR>I did the fronts several weeks ago.<P>My pedal is higher and firmer than it ever was with the problematic parking brake system.<BR>I believe that the 4 pounds (I weighed all the before components and all the after components) of unsprung weight per wheel that you gain is negligible, and is by far compensated for by the dramatically increased rigidity the iron calipers have over the aluminum calipers.<P>My 300 Yen.||||reg||1
